---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ContributionChecker from '../components/ContributionChecker.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import '../styles/catalog.css';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<Base
  title="Contribute to MailSchema"
  theme="light"
  description="Propose a type contract, improve MAP or contribute reproducible implementation evidence."
>
  <main id="main" class="container">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-line">Contribute</span>
      <h1>Bring a real interaction.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        What does your service ask someone to decide by email? Show the choices, what they affect
        and what happens if the proposal changes.
      </p>
    </header>
    <ContributionChecker />
    <div class="longform">
      <aside class="longform-aside">
        <span class="eyebrow">Contribution guide</span>
        <p>
          Type contracts
          <br />
          Specification changes
          <br />
          Implementation evidence
        </p>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="propose-a-type">Define the operation</h2>
        <p>
          Check the <a href="/registry">Registry</a> and existing standards first. A new type needs
          a distinct meaning that more than one service can implement. Name the effect precisely:
          approving a review is different from authorizing a send.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start with an existing JSON contract. Describe the required details, each choice’s
          meaning, effects, inputs and outcomes. Say which authority modes it permits. When a
          decision arrives, the service must reject it if any material term has changed. Keep
          endpoint paths and native request formats in the service binding.
        </p>
        <h2 id="review-the-draft">Submit for review</h2>
        <p>
          The browser checker validates and previews the contract locally. Continue in GitHub to
          propose the JSON file under <code>specifications/map-0.3/contracts/</code>. Include these
          companion changes in the same pull request:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A complete description in <code>examples/source.json</code>, keyed by the contract’s
            filename. Generation supplies the profile, context and contract digest.
          </li>
          <li>
            A record in <code>registry.json</code> naming its category and maintainers. Names,
            summaries and operations come directly from the contract.
          </li>
          <li>
            Relevant standards, motivation and implementation details in the pull-request
            description. Identify what an existing definition cannot express.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Generate and check the projections before review. On merge, the contract appears in the
          Registry, search, downloads and the reader’s type chapter. Adding a type does not require
          releasing every language package.
        </p>
        <h2 id="local-validation">Check a change locally</h2>
        <CodeBlock
          label="From the repository root"
          code={'npm ci\nnpm run spec:generate\nnpm run verify'}
        />
        <p>
          The browser and generator use the same contract parser and structural checks. Repository
          verification also checks examples, digests and generated artifacts. Passing those checks
          does not establish semantics, authority or compatibility with existing standards; review
          the contract and its example as well.
        </p>
        <h2 id="service-bindings">Connect an existing service</h2>
        <p>
          A service can implement an existing type without creating a new one. Supply its binding,
          exact supported contract digest, supported operations and host interface. Reuse the
          service's existing description and authorization. The
          <a href="/specification/bindings">binding guide</a> explains the required association; the{' '}
          <a href="/interfaces">interface research</a> covers existing standards and their gaps.
        </p>
        <p>
          Follow the <a href="/specification/registry">implementation record requirements</a> and
          include evidence with the contribution. An example mapping can show the idea; a Registry
          implementation claim needs a working service and steps another person can repeat. Listing
          an integration never installs it or grants account access.
        </p>
        <h2 id="implementation-evidence">Show what works</h2>
        <p>
          Submit a report with the exact contract ID, version and digest; implementation revision;
          service API mapping; supported operations; and reproduction instructions. Record actual
          observations for the <a href="/specification/conformance">conformance scenarios</a>,
          including failed checks and missing coverage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Demonstrate the service read and decision: a changed revision, a revoked permission and a
          competing decision must not produce an unauthorized effect. State who operates the
          implementation. A declaration of support is not an interoperability result.
        </p>
        <h2>Improve the specification</h2>
        <p>
          Point to the requirement and the concrete case it fails to handle. Include the observed or
          expected behaviour, and propose the smallest correction that resolves it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/mailschema/mailschema/issues/new">
            Open a specification issue →
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
